fact WorkItem(id: "WI-718", description: some(value: "Register `some`/`none` (and ideally all prelude constructors) as CONSTRUCTORS in register_prelude, so nullary-constructor discrim keying is stable from the start — closing a WI-697 order-dependence that WI-716 newly exposed.\n\nMECHANISM: a nullary constructor keys in the discrim tree as `Functor(c)+Arity(0)` BEFORE its sort registers it as a constructor, and canonicalizes to `Ref(c)` AFTER (the WI-511 alloc flip at kb/mod.rs:1074-1078 and the WI-436 functor_view_head at kb/term_view.rs:94 both gate on `is_constructor_symbol`). `is_constructor_symbol` is set only in `register_entity_of` (kb/mod.rs:2438) during the LOAD phase via `load_sort_with_body` — NOT in `register_prelude`, which defines the `none`/`some` symbols (kb/load.rs:2699/2712) but not the constructor flag. So `none` becomes a \"constructor\" only when option.anthill loads. WI-697 documented this pre/post-registration two-TermId-identity order-dependence.\n\nWHY WI-716 EXPOSES IT: the old omitted-field fresh-var fill was spelling-independent (a var unifies any spelling of `none`), so it was immune to the split. After WI-716 an omitted Option field is a CONCRETE `none()` whose stored key depends on whether `none` is a constructor at assert time. A ground fact with an omitted Option field asserted BEFORE option.anthill registers `none` would key its none-slot as `Functor(none)+Arity(0)` while later `f: none()` queries key `Ref(none)` — a SILENT discrim-tree miss (`resolve.rs` trusts the tree as complete).\n\nCURRENTLY NOT TRIGGERED (latent): files load alphabetically (anthill-cli main.rs `files.sort()`); the only pre-option.anthill facts are spec witnesses with no Option-typed fields (verified cell/effects/console/delay/external/list/logical_stream.anthill — Option appears only in operation RETURN types, never as an omitted entity/spec field); and user code always loads after the full prelude. So no realistic load order triggers it today.\n\nFIX: set `is_constructor_symbol` for `some`/`none` (and, more generally, for every prelude-defined constructor) at register_prelude time rather than only when their defining file loads, so the Fn{c}<->Ref(c) canonicalization is stable from the first assert. This also closes the general WI-697 order-dependence for these constructors independent of WI-716. (Broader: consider making is_constructor_symbol set at scan-pass-1 for ALL sort-body entities, so no fact can ever be asserted with a not-yet-constructor functor.)\n\nACCEPTANCE: a ground fact with an omitted Option field asserted BEFORE option.anthill loads (or under a partial / non-alphabetical load) is found by a `f: none()` query — no silent miss; a regression test exercising the pre-registration assert order; cargo-test green. Driver: adversarial review of WI-716 (reviewer-2 latent hazard); relates to WI-697."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-716"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-15T15:41:04Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-718", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (test-only; production fix already shipped). WI-718's requested change — mark some/none (and all prelude ctors) as constructors in register_prelude so the Fn{c,[],[]}->Ref(c) alloc/discrim canon is settled before the first assert — was ALREADY delivered under WI-719 (register_prelude_constructor at load.rs:2762-2763 for some/none, :2652-2653 for nil/cons) and generalized to EVERY sort-nested entity at scan pass 1 under WI-720 (mark_constructor_symbol at load.rs:1811-1813). The number split came from a WI-719 collision/renumber. So no production change was needed; the outstanding acceptance item was the regression test, which WI-719 shipped without and neither the WI-720 test (user enum, explicit red()) nor the WI-716 tests (full stdlib, benign order) covered for the prelude Option/none pre-registration order.\n\nAdded tests/include/wi718_prelude_ctor_preregistration_test.rs (commit 15322d6d), 3 tests, registered in wi_tests.rs:\n(1) register_prelude_marks_prelude_constructors — direct invariant: none/some/nil/cons answer is_constructor_symbol right after register_prelude, before any .anthill file loads. VERIFIED DISCRIMINATING: fails when WI-719's register_prelude_constructor calls are reverted (WI-720's scan marking cannot cover a register_prelude-only setup, so this isolates WI-719).\n(2) omitted_optional_fact_before_option_anthill_found_by_none_query — the literal acceptance: a fact omitting an Option field, converted before option.anthill (project source ordered ahead of stdlib), is found by a none() query (asserts exact id B). VERIFIED DISCRIMINATING: reproduces the exact WI-697 silent miss (Got 0) when BOTH WI-719 and WI-720's marking are reverted.\n(3) ..not_matched_by_some_query — WI-716 soundness dual (asserts exact id A).\n\nKEY INSIGHT recorded during impl: a register_prelude-ONLY behavioral test is NON-discriminating — both the fact's none() fill and the none() query build Fn{none} symmetrically, so they match whether or not none is a constructor. The WI-697 bug needs an ASYMMETRY (fact keys Fn early, query keys Ref late), which only arises when the constructor flag flips mid-load. Hence the pairing: a direct-invariant unit test for WI-719 + a front-loaded-order behavioral test for the end-to-end miss.\n\nFull workspace suite green (78 binaries, 2870 passed, 0 failed). Inline /code-review run; its one finding (cardinality-only assertions) fixed by asserting exact matched ids.", at: "2026-07-15T15:41:25Z")
